            <div xml:id="d774">

                <pb facs="0930" n="920" sameAs="frus:frus1944v02/pg920"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">840.70/12–2044: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the United Kingdom</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Winant</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-12-20T17:00:00+01:00">December 20,
                            1944—5 p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received December 20—1:34 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>11296. From EITO Delegation. Department’s 10400, December 13, 10 p.m. Embassy’s
                    11003, December 12, 1 p.m. was seen and <pb facs="0931" n="921" xml:id="pg921"/>
                    concurred in by Mosely. We had previously discussed with him the question of the
                    degree of authority to be exercised by each zonal commander independently of the
                    Control Council for Germany. Mosely agrees that, lacking unanimous agreement in
                    the Control Council regarding an economic matter affecting Germany as a whole,
                    each zonal commander would retain supreme authority over that matter within his
                    zone. He feels, however, that, in view of the probable transport needs both of
                    Europe and of Germany, unified control over German transport will be found to be
                    essential in the period of Allied control. Such matters of [<hi
                        rendition="#italic">as?</hi>] transfer of surplus equipment out of Germany
                    or the question of through service across the Soviet zone would, in any case,
                    require action by the central control machinery and hence the cooperation of the
                    Soviet Control element. He further feels that the Soviets, in view of their own
                    economic needs, attach very great importance to the German economy being
                    operated pretty much as a unit and that it would be difficult to operate any
                    branch of the German economy as a unit if the transportation system were broken
                    into three separate parts independently operated. [EITO Delegation.]</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Winant</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
